1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,866
An iron gate turns brown and flaky.

2
00:00:02,866 --> 00:00:05,132
A cut apple browns on the plate.

3
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:06,829
A gas flame burns blue.

4
00:00:07,229 --> 00:00:08,625
Three different things.

5
00:00:08,625 --> 00:00:10,764
One hidden swap behind all of them.

6
00:00:11,214 --> 00:00:13,791
Welcome to a new chapter, redox reactions.

7
00:00:13,791 --> 00:00:16,701
Aarav finds orange rust
on his old bicycle chain.

8
00:00:17,051 --> 00:00:20,523
Rain and air worked on the
iron, quietly, for months.

9
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:22,563
What did the iron lose?

10
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:27,058
By the end, you will see the tiny
thing that moves in every one of these.

11
00:00:27,708 --> 00:00:30,378
Long ago, chemists had a simple idea.

12
00:00:30,378 --> 00:00:33,353
Oxidation means adding
oxygen to a substance.

13
00:00:33,703 --> 00:00:36,962
Magnesium burns in air and
becomes magnesium oxide.

14
00:00:36,962 --> 00:00:38,581
Magnesium gained oxygen.

15
00:00:38,581 --> 00:00:39,754
It is oxidised.

16
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:43,047
Oxidation also means removing hydrogen.

17
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:48,081
Hydrogen sulphide meets oxygen, loses
its hydrogen, and leaves sulphur

18
00:00:48,081 --> 00:00:48,981
behind.

19
00:00:49,245 --> 00:00:51,264
Reduction is the opposite.

20
00:00:51,264 --> 00:00:53,746
Remove oxygen, or add hydrogen.

21
00:00:53,746 --> 00:00:59,082
Hot copper oxide gives its oxygen to
hydrogen, and shiny copper is left.

22
00:00:59,482 --> 00:01:02,358
In that same reaction,
hydrogen gains the oxygen.

23
00:01:02,358 --> 00:01:05,277
One is reduced, the other
is oxidised, together.

24
00:01:05,277 --> 00:01:07,259
Like a handshake, it takes two.

25
00:01:07,259 --> 00:01:08,433
We call it redox.

26
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:11,838
But many reactions have no oxygen at all.

27
00:01:11,838 --> 00:01:14,381
Let's watch one on our molecule bench.

28
00:01:14,381 --> 00:01:17,751
A zinc rod dips into blue
copper sulphate solution.

29
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:20,407
A zinc atom gives away two electrons.

30
00:01:20,407 --> 00:01:23,344
It leaves the rod as a
zinc ion and swims off.

31
00:01:23,744 --> 00:01:25,923
A blue copper ion catches
those two electrons.

32
00:01:25,923 --> 00:01:28,224
It becomes a copper atom
and sticks to the rod.

33
00:01:28,624 --> 00:01:29,734
Again and again.

34
00:01:29,734 --> 00:01:33,867
The rod gets a reddish copper coat,
and the blue colour slowly fades.

35
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:35,419
No oxygen moved.

36
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:38,109
Only electrons moved, from zinc to copper.

37
00:01:38,710 --> 00:01:41,792
So chemists gave a better
meaning, based on electrons.

38
00:01:42,142 --> 00:01:44,023
Oxidation is the loss of electrons.

39
00:01:44,023 --> 00:01:45,904
Reduction is the gain of electrons.

40
00:01:46,304 --> 00:01:48,488
Zinc lost electrons, so zinc is oxidised.

41
00:01:48,488 --> 00:01:51,066
Copper ions gained electrons,
so they are reduced.

42
00:01:51,066 --> 00:01:53,420
The old oxygen idea fits
inside this new one.

43
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:55,928
Now write it as two halves.

44
00:01:55,928 --> 00:01:56,953
Step by step.

45
00:01:57,303 --> 00:01:58,203
Step one.

46
00:01:58,081 --> 00:01:59,390
The oxidation half.

47
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:02,313
Zinc gives zinc two
plus, plus two electrons.

48
00:02:03,063 --> 00:02:03,963
Step two.

49
00:02:03,823 --> 00:02:05,102
The reduction half.

50
00:02:05,102 --> 00:02:08,280
Copper two plus, plus two
electrons, gives copper.

51
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:09,930
Step three.

52
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:11,074
Add the halves.

53
00:02:11,074 --> 00:02:12,767
The two electrons cancel.

54
00:02:12,767 --> 00:02:16,620
Zinc plus copper two plus gives
zinc two plus, plus copper.

55
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:19,454
Here is a trick to remember it.

56
00:02:19,454 --> 00:02:20,354
OIL RIG.

57
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:21,780
Oxidation is loss.

58
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:23,033
Reduction is gain.

59
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:24,522
Of electrons, always.

60
00:02:24,922 --> 00:02:27,013
Each half is called a half reaction.

61
00:02:27,013 --> 00:02:28,487
One half gives electrons.

62
00:02:28,487 --> 00:02:30,053
The other half takes them.

63
00:02:30,453 --> 00:02:31,995
Electrons never vanish.

64
00:02:31,995 --> 00:02:35,500
Every electron one atom
loses, another atom must gain.

65
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:38,630
Now compare the two helpers side by side.

66
00:02:38,630 --> 00:02:40,797
Think of a ball thrown in a game.

67
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:43,079
The reducing agent throws the ball.

68
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:45,862
It gives electrons away,
so it gets oxidised itself.

69
00:02:45,862 --> 00:02:46,762
Zinc is one.

70
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:48,896
The oxidising agent catches the ball.

71
00:02:48,896 --> 00:02:51,202
It takes electrons, so
it gets reduced itself.

72
00:02:51,202 --> 00:02:52,300
Copper ions are one.

73
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:53,878
Pause and think.

74
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:55,594
Magnesium burns in oxygen.

75
00:02:55,594 --> 00:02:57,692
Oxygen takes magnesium's electrons.

76
00:02:57,692 --> 00:02:59,216
Which agent is oxygen?

77
00:03:02,416 --> 00:03:03,630
The oxidising agent!

78
00:03:03,630 --> 00:03:07,629
Oxygen takes electrons, so oxygen is
reduced, and magnesium is oxidised.

79
00:03:08,279 --> 00:03:09,327
Remember this.

80
00:03:09,327 --> 00:03:13,844
Oxidation is loss, reduction is gain,
and they always happen together.

81
00:03:14,544 --> 00:03:16,234
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:16,534 --> 00:03:18,382
Sodium burns in chlorine gas.

83
00:03:18,382 --> 00:03:21,366
Two sodium plus chlorine
give two sodium chloride.

84
00:03:21,366 --> 00:03:23,981
Find what is oxidised and what is reduced.

85
00:03:24,481 --> 00:03:26,738
Each sodium atom loses one electron.

86
00:03:26,738 --> 00:03:30,006
Two sodium give two sodium
plus, plus two electrons.

87
00:03:30,006 --> 00:03:31,299
Sodium is oxidised.

88
00:03:32,049 --> 00:03:33,781
Chlorine takes those two electrons.

89
00:03:33,781 --> 00:03:36,252
Chlorine plus two electrons
give two chloride ions.

90
00:03:36,252 --> 00:03:37,329
Chlorine is reduced.

91
00:03:38,079 --> 00:03:43,164
So sodium is the reducing agent,
and chlorine is the oxidising agent.

92
00:03:43,914 --> 00:03:47,203
No oxygen anywhere, and
still a redox reaction.

93
00:03:47,203 --> 00:03:49,975
That is the power of the electron idea.

94
00:03:50,625 --> 00:03:52,087
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:03:52,437 --> 00:03:55,446
Students think the oxidising agent
is the one that gets oxidised.

96
00:03:55,446 --> 00:03:57,340
It sounds right, but it is backwards.

97
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:01,315
The oxidising agent oxidises
others, and gets reduced itself.

98
00:04:01,315 --> 00:04:03,882
The reducing agent is the other way round.

99
00:04:04,532 --> 00:04:05,974
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:05,974 --> 00:04:10,202
Boards ask you to define oxidation
and reduction, and split a reaction

101
00:04:10,202 --> 00:04:11,140
into halves.

102
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:13,878
Entrance tests ask you to spot the agents.

103
00:04:14,478 --> 00:04:15,687
Exam level now.

104
00:04:15,687 --> 00:04:20,348
Two iron three plus ions react with
two iodide ions to give two iron two

105
00:04:20,348 --> 00:04:21,926
plus ions and iodine.

106
00:04:21,926 --> 00:04:24,190
Which one is the reducing agent?

107
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:25,450
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:32,358
The answer is B.

109
00:04:32,358 --> 00:04:33,737
The iodide ion.

110
00:04:34,237 --> 00:04:36,357
Iodide ions lose electrons
and become iodine.

111
00:04:36,357 --> 00:04:37,322
They are oxidised.

112
00:04:37,822 --> 00:04:40,268
What gets oxidised is the reducing agent.

113
00:04:40,268 --> 00:04:43,964
Iron three plus gains electrons,
so it is the oxidising agent.

114
00:04:44,464 --> 00:04:46,261
A picks the oxidising agent.

115
00:04:46,261 --> 00:04:48,021
C and D are only products.

116
00:04:48,621 --> 00:04:50,161
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:50,161 --> 00:04:51,744
Screenshot this card.

118
00:04:54,944 --> 00:04:57,130
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:04:57,530 --> 00:04:58,430
One.

120
00:04:57,953 --> 00:05:00,076
Define oxidation in terms of electrons.

121
00:05:00,876 --> 00:05:01,776
Two.

122
00:05:01,326 --> 00:05:04,495
When zinc meets copper
sulphate, which one is reduced?

123
00:05:05,295 --> 00:05:06,195
Three.

124
00:05:05,793 --> 00:05:08,349
Does the oxidising agent
gain or lose electrons?

125
00:05:09,249 --> 00:05:13,371
Next time: how do we track electrons
when nobody gives them away fully?

126
00:05:13,371 --> 00:05:15,317
We find out in oxidation number.
